# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH — every env var for the whole stack lives in
# this one file. Copy it to `.env` and fill in real values. There are
# NO per-profile `.env` files; compose maps these vars
# into each service as needed.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── LLM Provider API Key (VPS-ONLY secret — never committed) ──────
# Real key lives ONLY in the git-ignored root `.env` on the VPS.
# It belongs to T3/opencode on the host (the agent no longer runs in a
# container), and the kept MCP servers read only the two Mongo variables.
# Provider + model are picked per request in the app Settings. This file
# carries a placeholder only.
OPENCODE_API_KEY=sk-...

# ── Android app password (single credential for sync) ──────────────
# PASSWORD is the bearer credential health-api accepts and the Android
# app sends on every call. Must match the Password field in Agento
# Settings. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
PASSWORD=change-me

# ── Single app URL (reverse proxy for sync) ─────────────────────────
# The proxy routes /api/* + /health → health-api :8000. Point the Agento
# app's Server URL here: http://<host>:8080. Direct health-api
# (:8001 — compose default; HEALTH_HOST_PORT remaps the host side) stays
# published for comparison.
# APP_PORT=8080                   # override (compose default: 8080)
# HEALTH_HOST_PORT=8001           # override (compose default: 8001)

# ── Remote MongoDB (Atlas: money/health/cookbook domain data) ───────
MONGODB_URI=mongodb+srv://user:pass@cluster0.example.mongodb.net/
MONGODB_DB=hermes

# ── Google connectors — OAuth + envelope key (#316) ────────────────
# POST /api/connectors/{id}/connect returns an auth_url the app opens in a
# browser; Google redirects to the single registered callback
# GET /api/connectors/callback?code=&state=, the server exchanges + stores
# encrypted tokens, and the connector flips to connected. Register this ONE
# redirect URI in the Google Cloud console (OAuth client, Web application).
# The path needs NO profile segment: health-api serves the callback
# directly (the retired pi-gateway accepted a /p/ first segment, which
# went with #494; register the bare spelling for health-api), so
# register exactly one spelling — Google only ever sees the
# one registered string.
# (copy verbatim, host included). Public spellings only: dotted FQDN or
# loopback — LAN/mDNS names, non-loopback IPs, and Unicode hosts fail closed
# by design (register the punycode form, never Unicode; homelab: nip.io /
# Tailscale funnel / reverse proxy for a public name, or SSH tunnel to
# localhost for direct-only). Full rules in documentation.md (connectors section).
#   https://app.example.com/api/connectors/callback
#   http://localhost:8001/api/connectors/callback   (localhost shown as one shape among equals: 127.0.0.1 and [::1] follow the same shape — substitute the host — but each spelling is a DISTINCT registration, so register the one the browser actually reaches. `:8001` is the mapped direct host port (`HEALTH_HOST_PORT`, compose default 8001, reaching health-api direct); `:8080` is the proxy publish (`APP_PORT`, compose default 8080, reaching it through the proxy) — distinct registrations, register the one the browser reaches. The :8080 proxy publish is plain HTTP: only loopback http://localhost:8080/... registers, never a public https://...:8080 spelling.)
#   (IPv6 loopback: http://[::1]:8001/api/connectors/callback — brackets stay with the port outside, never [::1][:8001].)
# (omit :443/:80 — canonicalisation strips default ports. Register the
# spelling the browser actually reaches — direct :8001, or loopback-only
# http://localhost:8080 through the proxy (public https://...:8080 serves
# nothing) — "only" as registration guidance (Google exact-matches one
# string), not server routing. IPv6 literals keep brackets with the port outside.)
# Use https except for loopback: plain http to any other host fails
# closed (Google only permits http for loopback).
# Config rotations (redirect, client secret) invalidate in-flight attempts:
# a rotation between connect and callback strands that attempt — re-tap
# connect for a fresh one. Rotating the envelope key below invalidates
# every stored grant: all connectors need reconnect afterwards. Runbook:
# rows keep reading `connected` (decrypt only happens on use, surfacing as
# a tool error — the list never flips itself to expired), so reconnect each
# connector once even though nothing banners — that is expected key
# fallout, not a provider outage. The boot `configured` comparison below
# is live-config only: mid-consent after a redirect rotation the sent bytes
# are the pinned pre-rotation string (health-api logs `uses pinned
# redirect` per callback) — re-tap connect rather than re-registering.
# Whichever spelling you register, GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URL below must equal the
# canonical string in health-api's startup log byte-for-byte (`logs
# health-api | grep -E "OAuth (configured|unavailable|redirect invalid)" —
# the narrowed pattern skips the "boot check started" starter line, so poll
# until a status line lands instead of grepping once): the log prints the
# canonical form ("OAuth configured ... (callback ...)") — copy THAT string
# into the console, not your input spelling, since lowercasing, dot-trimming,
# and default-port stripping can all move the sent bytes. The configured line
# logs the exact sent bytes, never truncated: CanonicalRedirectURL rejects
# anything over 2048 bytes, so an over-long value fails as `OAuth redirect
# invalid` instead of a truncatable line.
# Google exact-matches redirect_uri, so a registered-vs-sent mismatch dies with
# redirect_uri_mismatch in the browser while every server-side check
# passes. Omit default ports (:443 for https, :80 for http) — registering
# https://h:443/... verbatim mismatches the stored https://h/.... Ports
# canonicalise numerically too: :080 sends as :80, so register the canonical
# form from the startup log, never the zero-padded spelling.
# Without all four values — or without a durable store (Mongo unreachable
# falls back to memory, and memory is not durable for grants) — connect
# answers 501 and no token can be stored. The fallback is not instant on a
# post-boot drop: the first few taps 502 while the demotion counter trips
# (3 upstream failures inside a minute AND more than a minute since the
# last probe — boot and recovery both stamp it, so a drop right after
# either 502s until that minute expires, not just 3 taps), then it flips
# to memory + 501 — so a 502 right after an Atlas loss means Mongo, not
# Google.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=...            # OAuth client ID (public in the consent URL by design — secrecy buys nothing, correctness does)
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=...         # OAuth client secret (VPS-ONLY secret — never committed)
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URL=...         # exact registered callback URL above
# Envelope key for OAuth tokens at rest (AES-256-GCM, server-side only).
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32. Missing = OAuth unconfigured.
# CONNECTORS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=...
